//! THE BOOT HANDOFF (spec §12) — the boot-handle record and the boot handle set.
//!
//! The three-word record a `Give` writes and a `BootHandleNext` hands back, the
//! kinds it can name, and the two constants the kernel's own handoff is
//! written in.
//!
//! Reached through the `sosabi` facade — see `lib.saw`.

// =============================================================================
// The boot-handle record (spec §12; sawos design 2 D-3, settling sawlang#232
// agenda item 5)
// =============================================================================
//
// `Process.BootHandleNext` answers with a RECORD the kernel copies out, on the
// `Waiter.Wait` precedent exactly and through the same funnel. Three machine
// words: which region this is, what kind of object was minted for it, and the
// handle.
//
// IT IS MACHINE WORDS, NOT DOUBLEWORDS, and the rule is the wait record's: a
// tag, a kind and a handle are all machine-word-shaped things, so the layout
// follows the register. Only a TIME is a fixed 64 bits on every profile.
//
// THE RECORD IS CONSUMED BY DELIVERY, AND THERE ARE TWO DELIVERIES. The drain
// hands back the next undelivered record and advances; there is no rewind, no
// peek and no count op. A drained iterator answers `SosStatus.Drained` forever —
// a loop's termination check, never a fault, because exhaustion is exactly what
// a caller could not have known in advance and is the ordinary way the loop
// ends. **THE OTHER DELIVERY IS `start(boot_tag:)`'s RESOLUTION** (the Aug-29
// user ruling): putting a record's word in the child's first argument REGISTER
// is a delivery like any other, so that record is spent and the drain skips it.
// One record, one delivery, whichever door it went out of — which is what makes
// "a child can never be handed one word twice" a property of the kernel rather
// than an obligation on the child.
//
// **THE SET IS PER-PROCESS SINCE M3 UNIT 3** (sawos design 4 D-2), and there are
// now two ways one gets filled: the KERNEL writes root's at boot, one record per
// row of the build-emitted region table, and a LAUNCHER writes a child's with
// `ProcessOp.Give`, one record per capability it hands across. Nothing about the
// record or the op distinguishes them — the kernel is the giver nobody gave to.
// The set FREEZES at `Start` (a give afterwards is a `BadState` fault), which is
// what makes a half-populated table unrepresentable at the child's first
// instruction with no synchronization invented; the DRAIN has no deadline at
// all, so a child that drains late drains correctly.

/// Words in a boot-handle record: tag, kind, handle.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS: Int = 3

/// Where each lives. Named rather than written, on both sides of the boundary.
public static BOOT_HANDLE_TAG_WORD: Int = 0
public static BOOT_HANDLE_KIND_WORD: Int = 1
public static BOOT_HANDLE_HANDLE_WORD: Int = 2

/// Bytes in a boot-handle record — the minimum capacity a caller must give the
/// op, and what the kernel checks the buffer against.
///
/// A FUNCTION AND NOT A `static` for the reason `wait_record_bytes` gives:
/// `sizeof<T>()` does not fold in a static initializer (DF-178f).
public func boot_handle_record_bytes() -> Int {
    BOOT_HANDLE_RECORD_WORDS * sizeof<UInt>()
}

/// What kind of object a boot-handle record names — the record's KIND WORD.
///
/// PUBLIC API rather than kernel bookkeeping, on the module docstring's line:
/// the kernel WRITES this value and userspace READS it, so one declaration has
/// to serve both. It is deliberately NOT `ObjType`, which is kernel-internal
/// and renumberable — a boot handle's kind crosses the trap boundary, so it is
/// its own closed set with its own stated numbers.
///
/// M3 unit 2 declared one kind, because the boot set was exactly the region
/// table's rows. **UNIT 3 DECLARES THE SECOND** (sawos design 4 D-3): a give may
/// carry a PROCESS handle, and the one that matters is the child's own — the
/// drain lives on the Process object, so a child that is to drain must be handed
/// the handle `process_create` minted. The raw backing is what lets a kind be
/// ADDED without renumbering (design 145's wire idiom), which is exactly what
/// happened here and happened AGAIN at unit 4's `IoMemory` below; unit 5.5's
/// death channels are the next to want it.
///
/// **THE SET IS EXACTLY THE GIVABLE KINDS, and it is smaller than `ObjType` on
/// purpose.** A kind reaches this enum when something can actually deliver one:
/// v1 mints the universal `Transfer` bit in `memory_rights()`, in
/// `child_process_rights()` (D-3) and — since the Aug-29 rulings — in
/// `root_system_rights()`, and NOWHERE ELSE. So a Thread, Event, Waiter,
/// Interrupt, Clock or Timer handle cannot be given at all and a case for one
/// would be dead surface — the same rule `ClockType` follows in declaring one
/// domain. The kernel's `boot_kind_of` is the one place the two enums meet, and
/// it refuses what it cannot spell rather than inventing a number.
///
/// `System` is the one a launch actually uses: it is what a child receives in
/// its first argument register, a masked sibling of root's own, which is what
/// makes every process bootstrap the same way (§12).
///
/// Backed by `UInt`, the width of the word it travels in, for the reason
/// `WaitTag` gives: userspace decodes it through `from(raw:)`, which is total.
/// **THE TAG BESIDE IT IS THE GIVER'S OWN WORD, HANDED BACK UNREAD.** For root's
/// boot set the kernel is the giver and the tags are region ordinals (row `n` is
/// tag `n`, sawos design 2 D-3); for a child's set the LAUNCHER is the giver and
/// the tag is whatever it chose. The kernel knows nothing about either — which
/// ordinal is an image blob, which is destination RAM, which capability a child
/// expects under 7 — because THE TAG IS THE IDENTITY and identities are the only
/// cross-process vocabulary there is (design 4's ruled surface). Keeping the
/// meaning in root's config is what makes the kernel's half of the launch flow
/// the same code for a hand-built M3 system and for M4's dynamic loading.
/// **UNIT 4 DECLARES THE FOURTH** (sawos design 6 D-1/D-5), which is the
/// raw backing doing exactly what the paragraph above predicted: the region
/// table now carries a KIND column, a Device row mints an `IoMemory`, and root
/// receives it through the same drain as everything else. It is `Transfer`-able
/// (`iomemory_rights()`) because handing a driver child its window is unit 6's
/// whole flow. **THAT FLOW IS BUILT** (sawos design 9 D-2): root drains the
/// console's window at boot and GIVES it to a child that maps it into itself and
/// echoes the harness's bytes, on both profiles — so this case is now exercised
/// end to end rather than merely declared.
///
/// There is deliberately NO `Mapping` case. A Mapping is not givable —
/// `mapping_rights()` withholds `Transfer` on purpose (see `MappingRight`) — so
/// a case for one would be dead surface on the rule this set already follows.
public enum BootHandleKind: UInt {
    case Memory = 0,
    case Process = 1,
    case System = 2,
    case IoMemory = 3,
    /// **M4 UNIT 1's PAIR, AND THE RAW BACKING DOING WHAT IT KEEPS DOING**
    /// (sawos design 13 D-3): two more givable kinds took the next two numbers
    /// and moved none of the four above them. `pipe_inlet_rights()` and
    /// `pipe_outlet_rights()` mint `Transfer` because handing a child ONE END of
    /// a connection is the whole point of the pair — a launcher creates it and
    /// wires the two ends outward, which is what makes `pipe-child` the seed of
    /// unit 5's driver-as-service.
    case PipeInlet = 4,
    case PipeOutlet = 5,
    /// **M4 UNIT 2's ONE-SHOTS, AND THIS PAIR IS THE ONE §2.1 ARGUED FOR BY
    /// NAME** (sawos design 14). `pipe_reply_rights()` and
    /// `pipe_request_rights()` mint `Transfer` because the ratified section says
    /// both one-shots are transferable and calls it THE DELEGATION PRIMITIVE: a
    /// server MOVES its obligation to a third party, who replies straight to the
    /// original client, and the payload never transits the middle process. So a
    /// give must be able to carry one, and a give's record must be able to spell
    /// it.
    case PipeReply = 6,
    case PipeRequest = 7,
}

// THE DECODED RECORD IS NOT DECLARED HERE, and the asymmetry with `WaitResult`
// is worth one note. A wait answer is made of plain words — a key, a tag, a
// count — so one declaration serves the kernel that writes it and the process
// that reads it. A boot-handle answer carries a HANDLE, and the whole point of
// the `sos` module's typed layer is that a handle a process holds is a `Memory`
// or a `Process` and never a word. So the decoded form is `sos`'s
// `BootHandle`, which this file could not name; what crosses is the three-word
// layout above, and that is declared once, here.

// =============================================================================
// The boot handle set (spec §12)
// =============================================================================

/// The handle the kernel gives root for the System object. Root receives it in
/// the first argument register at entry rather than assuming it, so this
/// constant is the KERNEL's choice of what to mint — not a number userspace is
/// expected to know.
public static ROOT_SYSTEM_HANDLE: UInt = 1

/// The `stack_top` handed to a user entry that was granted no stack region.
public static NO_USER_STACK: UInt = 0
